Why it is vendored rather than installed from PyPI
--------------------------------------------------
The OpenMax detector used by this benchmark carries a local change: its
constructor takes an extra `epsilon` argument
(`detector/openmax/torch.py`), which the evaluation loop reads back as
`detector.epsilon` to threshold known/unknown decisions. Installing stock
pytorch-ood from PyPI would not provide it.

Why it is renamed
-----------------
The package is imported as `osr_pytorch_ood`, not `pytorch_ood`. Vendoring
under the upstream name is not enough to guarantee this code is the code that
runs: any `pytorch_ood` reachable from sys.path — a PyPI install, or a
directory added by a stray .pth file — shadows a vendored copy, silently.
That happened on the machine these experiments were run on, which is why the
original scripts carried a sys.path filter. A distinct name removes the
possibility entirely.

Known local change
------------------
- detector/openmax/torch.py: the `epsilon` constructor argument and the
  `self.epsilon` attribute are not part of the upstream OpenMax API.
- Absolute self-imports were rewritten from `pytorch_ood.*` to
  `osr_pytorch_ood.*` to match the renamed package.

This directory is the copy the reported results were produced with, and is
what the benchmark imports. A full file-by-file diff against the upstream
0.2.2 release has not been performed, so this tree — not PyPI — is
authoritative for reproducing the OpenMax numbers.

Files that were local additions rather than upstream code (plotting and
metrics helpers under utils/, and dataset/img/CarregaImagens.py) have been
removed, along with their imports in utils/__init__.py. Removing them was
verified not to change the OpenMax results.
